※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `⎕heap` is an allocator that gives back one object at a time, in the runtime: room is rounded
※ to a class -- sixteen bytes apart up to 256, then powers of two up to 64 KiB -- each class keeps
※ what was given back to it, and anything larger is mapped on its own.  Giving back takes the
※ size, which the compiler always knows, so no object carries a header saying it.
※
※ This grows one string past every class and well past the largest, and makes enough small
※ ones to run through more than one slab.

※ Each turn joins a new string and the old one stays where it was -- nothing gives back
※ what a local held -- so the turns are few and long: seventy of a thousand bytes come to
※ 70000, past the largest class, and to about two and a half megabytes in all.
fn grown(n: u64) → u64:
    let piece: mut str = ""
    let j: mut u64 = 0u64
    while j < 125u64:
        piece ← piece ⧺ "abcdefgh"
        j ← j + 1u64
    let s: mut str = ""
    let i: mut u64 = 0u64
    while i < n:
        s ← s ⧺ piece
        i ← i + 1u64
    ⎕drop(#s)

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    if grown(70u64) ≠ 70000u64:
        return 1u6
    let k: mut u64 = 0u64
    while k < 20000u64:
        let t: str = "x" ⧺ "yz"
        if t ≠ "xyz":
            return 2u6
        k ← k + 1u64
    0u6
